        "heading": "5 DISCUSSION",
        "text": "In this work, we proposed a novel explanation technique that attributes decisions suggested by an RL agent to trajectories encountered by the agent in the past. We provided an algorithm that enables us to perform trajectory attribution in offline RL. The key idea behind our approach was to encode trajectories using sequence modelling techniques, cluster the trajectories using these embeddings and then study the sensitivity of original RL agent\u2019s policy to the trajectories present in each of these clusters. We demonstrated the utility of our method using experiments in grid-world, Seaquest and HalfCheetah environments. In the end, we also presented a human evaluation of the results of our method to further underline the necessity of trajectory attribution.\nThe ideas presented in this paper, such as generating trajectory embedding using sequence encoders, creating an encoding of the set of trajectories, can be extended to other domains of RL. For instance, the trajectory embeddings could be beneficial in recognizing hierarchical behaviour patterns as studied under options theory (Sutton et al., 1999). The data encoding could prove useful in studying transfer learning (Zhu et al., 2020) in RL by utilizing the data embeddings from both the source and target decision-making tasks. As from the XRL point of view, we wish to extend our work to online RL settings where the agent constantly collects experiences through active interactions with the environment.\nWhile all our experiments are very promising, we need to be cautious about the ability of sequential modelling techniques to learn complex temporal patterns, especially in problems involving highdimensional, multi-modal observations and/or actions. Also, the training of multiple explanation policies could pose challenges due to extra computational requirements."
